---
import Layout from "../_components/Layout.bangala"
---
<Layout title="Adapters | bangala.js">
  <div class="docs-layout">
    <aside class="docs-sidebar">
      <h3>Getting Started</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/docs">Introduction</a></li>
        <li><a href="/docs/installation">Installation</a></li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Core Concepts</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/docs/islands">Islands Architecture</a></li>
        <li><a href="/docs/routing">File-based Routing</a></li>
        <li><a href="/docs/syntax">.bangala Syntax</a></li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Deployment</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/docs/adapters" class="active">Adapters</a></li>
      </ul>
    </aside>
    <div class="docs-content">
      <p class="section-label">Deployment</p>
      <h1>Adapters</h1>
      <p class="section-sub">bangala.js builds static HTML &mdash; nothing more. You can serve the output from any static host, or wire bangala into a server runtime as a library. The built-in adapters write the right config for the most common hosts.</p>

      <h2>Deployment overview</h2>
      <p>Running <code>bangala build</code> prerenders every route in <code>pages/</code> to plain HTML and bundles <code>bangala/client/auto</code> for island hydration. The output lands in <code>dist/</code> by default. There is no server runtime to deploy: just upload the directory to the host of your choice.</p>
      <p>For convenience, <code>bangala deploy &lt;adapter&gt;</code> writes the configuration file that tells your host how to build and serve the project. The available adapters are <code>static</code>, <code>netlify</code>, <code>vercel</code>, and <code>cloudflare-pages</code>. You can also call <code>createDeployAdapter()</code> and <code>applyDeployAdapter()</code> programmatically from <code>bangala/adapters</code>.</p>

      <h2>Vercel</h2>
      <p>The Vercel adapter writes a minimal <code>vercel.json</code> at the project root telling Vercel which command to run and which directory to serve:</p>
      <div class="hero-code" style="margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem;">
        <div class="code-header">
          <div class="dots"><div class="dot dot-r"></div><div class="dot dot-y"></div><div class="dot dot-g"></div></div>
          <span class="code-fname">Terminal</span>
        </div>
        <div class="code-body">
<span class="ln">1</span><span class="kw">npx</span> <span class="fn">bangala</span> deploy <span class="str">vercel</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-code" style="margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem;">
        <div class="code-header">
          <div class="dots"><div class="dot dot-r"></div><div class="dot dot-y"></div><div class="dot dot-g"></div></div>
          <span class="code-fname">vercel.json</span>
        </div>
        <div class="code-body">
<span class="ln">1</span>&#123;
<span class="ln">2</span>  <span class="str">"framework"</span>: <span class="kw">null</span>,
<span class="ln">3</span>  <span class="str">"buildCommand"</span>: <span class="str">"npm run build"</span>,
<span class="ln">4</span>  <span class="str">"outputDirectory"</span>: <span class="str">"dist"</span>
<span class="ln">5</span>&#125;
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>Then connect the repo on Vercel; the platform will run <code>npm run build</code> and serve <code>dist/</code>.</p>

      <h2>Netlify</h2>
      <p>The Netlify adapter writes a <code>netlify.toml</code> at the project root. It declares the build command, the publish directory, and a sensible <code>X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff</code> header on every response:</p>
      <div class="hero-code" style="margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem;">
        <div class="code-header">
          <div class="dots"><div class="dot dot-r"></div><div class="dot dot-y"></div><div class="dot dot-g"></div></div>
          <span class="code-fname">Terminal</span>
        </div>
        <div class="code-body">
<span class="ln">1</span><span class="kw">npx</span> <span class="fn">bangala</span> deploy <span class="str">netlify</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-code" style="margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem;">
        <div class="code-header">
          <div class="dots"><div class="dot dot-r"></div><div class="dot dot-y"></div><div class="dot dot-g"></div></div>
          <span class="code-fname">netlify.toml</span>
        </div>
        <div class="code-body">
<span class="ln">1</span>[build]
<span class="ln">2</span>  command = <span class="str">"npm run build"</span>
<span class="ln">3</span>  publish = <span class="str">"dist"</span>
<span class="ln">4</span>
<span class="ln">5</span>[[headers]]
<span class="ln">6</span>  <span class="kw">for</span> = <span class="str">"/*"</span>
<span class="ln">7</span>  [headers.values]
<span class="ln">8</span>    X-Content-Type-Options = <span class="str">"nosniff"</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Cloudflare Pages</h2>
      <p>The Cloudflare Pages adapter writes a <code>wrangler.toml</code> with the project name and build output directory:</p>
      <div class="hero-code" style="margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem;">
        <div class="code-header">
          <div class="dots"><div class="dot dot-r"></div><div class="dot dot-y"></div><div class="dot dot-g"></div></div>
          <span class="code-fname">Terminal</span>
        </div>
        <div class="code-body">
<span class="ln">1</span><span class="kw">npx</span> <span class="fn">bangala</span> deploy <span class="str">cloudflare-pages</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-code" style="margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem;">
        <div class="code-header">
          <div class="dots"><div class="dot dot-r"></div><div class="dot dot-y"></div><div class="dot dot-g"></div></div>
          <span class="code-fname">wrangler.toml</span>
        </div>
        <div class="code-body">
<span class="ln">1</span>name = <span class="str">"bangala-app"</span>
<span class="ln">2</span>pages_build_output_dir = <span class="str">"dist"</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>The shortcuts <code>cloudflare</code> and <code>cf-pages</code> are accepted as aliases for <code>cloudflare-pages</code>.</p>

      <h2>Node.js / standalone</h2>
      <p>The static adapter is a no-op &mdash; it doesn&rsquo;t write a config file, it just confirms that uploading <code>dist/</code> to any static host is the whole deployment story. Use it as documentation, or skip it entirely:</p>
      <div class="hero-code" style="margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem;">
        <div class="code-body">
<span class="ln">1</span><span class="kw">npx</span> <span class="fn">bangala</span> deploy <span class="str">static</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>If you want to embed bangala in an existing Node.js server (Express, Hono, Fastify, etc.), call the compiler programmatically: <code>compile()</code> turns a <code>.bangala</code> source into an ESM module, and the generated <code>render(props)</code> returns an HTML string you can pipe straight to the response. Server-side rendering on demand &mdash; without the build step &mdash; is not part of the v1.0 adapter surface, but the compiler is a public API you can wire up yourself.</p>
      <p>Routing helpers (<code>discoverRoutes</code>, <code>matchRoute</code>) are also exported from <code>bangala/router</code> so a custom server can resolve URLs the same way the build does.</p>

      <h2>GitHub Pages</h2>
      <p>GitHub Pages doesn&rsquo;t need an adapter file at all &mdash; it just needs a workflow that runs the build and uploads <code>dist/</code> as a Pages artifact. The bangala.js docs site you&rsquo;re reading right now is deployed exactly this way:</p>
      <div class="hero-code" style="margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem;">
        <div class="code-header">
          <div class="dots"><div class="dot dot-r"></div><div class="dot dot-y"></div><div class="dot dot-g"></div></div>
          <span class="code-fname">.github/workflows/deploy-docs.yml</span>
        </div>
        <div class="code-body">
<span class="ln">1</span>- <span class="kw">name</span>: Build site
<span class="ln">2</span>  <span class="kw">run</span>: node ./dist/cli.js build --pages docs --out-dir _site
<span class="ln">3</span>
<span class="ln">4</span>- <span class="kw">name</span>: Upload artifact
<span class="ln">5</span>  <span class="kw">uses</span>: actions/upload-pages-artifact@v3
<span class="ln">6</span>  <span class="kw">with</span>:
<span class="ln">7</span>    path: _site
<span class="ln">8</span>
<span class="ln">9</span>- <span class="kw">name</span>: Deploy
<span class="ln">10</span>  <span class="kw">uses</span>: actions/deploy-pages@v4
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>The same recipe works for any project: install dependencies, run <code>bangala build</code>, upload the output, deploy. No dedicated adapter is needed because GitHub Pages serves any static directory you hand it.</p>

      <h2>Roadmap</h2>
      <p>Server-side adapters (SSR on Vercel Functions, Cloudflare Workers, Node middleware) are deliberately out of scope for v1.0 &mdash; the build is static-first. If you have a use case that needs runtime rendering, file an issue: it&rsquo;s the next major area of the framework to explore.</p>

      <div class="cta-actions" style="margin-top: 3rem; justify-content: flex-start;">
        <a href="/docs" class="btn-primary">Back to Introduction</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
<style>
.docs-layout { display: flex; max-width: 1200px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 4rem 2rem; gap: 4rem; align-items: flex-start; }
.docs-sidebar { width: 240px; flex-shrink: 0; position: sticky; top: 100px; }
.docs-sidebar h3 { font-size: 0.85rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 1px; color: var(--text3); margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
.docs-sidebar ul { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; }
.docs-sidebar li { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
.docs-sidebar a { color: var(--text2); text-decoration: none; font-size: 0.95rem; transition: color 0.2s; }
.docs-sidebar a:hover, .docs-sidebar a.active { color: var(--accent); }
.docs-content { flex-grow: 1; max-width: 760px; }
.docs-content h1 { font-size: 3rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; letter-spacing: -1px; }
.docs-content h2 { font-size: 1.8rem; margin-top: 3rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; letter-spacing: -0.5px; }
.docs-content p { color: var(--text2); font-size: 1.1rem; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
.docs-content code { background: var(--bg2); padding: 0.2em 0.4em; border-radius: 4px; font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.9em; color: var(--accent); border: 1px solid var(--border); }
@media (max-width: 768px) { .docs-layout { flex-direction: column; gap: 2rem; } .docs-sidebar { width: 100%; position: static; } }
</style>
